47
00:02:55,851 --> 00:02:56,627
She was 19.

48
00:02:57,459 --> 00:02:58,435
England lost anyway.

49
00:02:59,404 --> 00:03:04,471
Then came the man who built the
system, Louis XIV, the Sun King.

50
00:03:05,082 --> 00:03:08,503
He ruled for 72 years, the
longest reign in European history.

51
00:03:08,548 --> 00:03:12,945
As a child, he lived through a rebellion
by his own nobles, and he never forgot

52
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:18,517
it, so he built Versailles, moved
thousands of courtiers in, and had the

53
00:03:18,519 --> 00:03:23,418
greatest aristocrats in France compete
for the honor of handing him his shirt.

54
00:03:23,956 --> 00:03:27,156
If your rival was busy fighting
over your shirt, they have

55
00:03:27,156 --> 00:03:28,627
no time to plot against you.

56
00:03:28,926 --> 00:03:32,999
He never said, "I am the state." He
just ran the country as if he had.

57
00:03:33,572 --> 00:03:36,855
But Versailles was
expensive, so were his wars.

58
00:03:37,158 --> 00:03:42,216
He died in 1715, leaving enormous
debts, and that bill didn't disappear.

59
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:47,321
It got handed down the family tree
with interest for 70 years until it

60
00:03:47,321 --> 00:03:52,077
landed on the desk of a side man whose
favorite hobby was making locks and

61
00:03:52,088 --> 00:03:59,418
who had absolutely no idea how to
fix a budget His name was Louis XVI.

62
00:03:59,727 --> 00:04:05,259
He became king at 19 in 1774, and by
almost every account, he was a decent,

63
00:04:05,472 --> 00:04:09,691
well-meaning, slightly awkward man
who'd rather have in his own workshop.

64
00:04:10,497 --> 00:04:15,767
His wife was Maria, an Austrian princess
who arrived in France as a teenager and

65
00:04:15,767 --> 00:04:18,315
was disliked at court almost immediately.

66
00:04:18,728 --> 00:04:20,336
Here is how money worked in his kingdom.

67
00:04:20,555 --> 00:04:24,496
French society was split into
three groups, called estates.

68
00:04:25,002 --> 00:04:26,371
The clergy were the first.

69
00:04:26,538 --> 00:04:27,926
The nobility were the second.

70
00:04:28,181 --> 00:04:32,513
Everyone else, about 98% of the
whole country, were the third estate.

71
00:04:32,757 --> 00:04:37,344
And the first were too largely
exempt from the biggest direct tax.

72
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:41,683
So the richest people in the kingdom paid
the least, and the poorest paid the most.

73
00:04:42,495 --> 00:04:46,616
Then the king made it worse by backing
the American Revolution to hurt Britain.

74
00:04:47,291 --> 00:04:52,085
France helped America win independence,
which cost more than a billion lives,

75
00:04:52,239 --> 00:04:54,356
and in exchange, they got bankrupt.

76
00:04:54,808 --> 00:04:59,399
By 1780s, roughly half of the royal
budget was going on interest alone.

77
00:04:59,847 --> 00:05:02,185
That's like spending half of
your salary on the minimum

78
00:05:02,186 --> 00:05:04,221
payment on a credit card forever.

79
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:08,927
Finance minister tried to fix it
by taxing the privileged every

80
00:05:08,928 --> 00:05:10,523
single time the privileged broke it.

81
00:05:11,053 --> 00:05:14,801
In 1787, the king even called
Assembly of Nobiles to approve

82
00:05:14,802 --> 00:05:16,967
reforms, and they said no.

83
00:05:17,447 --> 00:05:18,644
So the system was locked.

84
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:22,534
The people with money wouldn't pay,
and the people without money could not.

85
00:05:23,029 --> 00:05:23,974
Then nature piled on.

86
00:05:24,528 --> 00:05:29,892
In July 1788, a freak hailstorm
destroyed crops across northern France.

87
00:05:30,948 --> 00:05:33,857
Then came one of the coldest
winters anyone could remember.

88
00:05:34,316 --> 00:05:39,796
Grain collapsed, bread prices spiked,
and by the summer of 1789, by some

89
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:44,832
estimates, a laborer was spending up
to 88% of his wages on bread alone.

90
00:05:45,438 --> 00:05:48,760
And no, Maria never said,
"Let them eat the cake."

91
00:05:49,462 --> 00:05:54,044
That line appears in Rochet Confesses,
written when she was a child in Austria.

92
00:05:54,331 --> 00:05:58,137
It was a story people told
about out-of-touch royals

93
00:05:58,195 --> 00:05:59,416
long before she arrived.

94
00:05:59,961 --> 00:06:03,999
It stuck because it felt true,
and in a crisis what often

95
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,743
feels matters than what it is.

96
00:06:07,294 --> 00:06:11,331
By 1788, the king was out of
options, so he did something no

97
00:06:11,332 --> 00:06:14,570
French king had done for 175 years.

98
00:06:14,847 --> 00:06:16,027
He asked the people.

99
00:06:16,108 --> 00:06:18,763
He had no idea that he was
calling the meeting that would

100
00:06:18,808 --> 00:06:20,967
end his own dynasty forever.

101
00:06:22,020 --> 00:06:26,371
The States General opened in
Versailles on fifth May 1789.

102
00:06:26,549 --> 00:06:27,874
Three estates, one hall.

103
00:06:28,264 --> 00:06:33,580
The third estates had been granted double
the deputies, about 600 against roughly

104
00:06:33,595 --> 00:06:36,119
300 each for the clergy and the nobility.

105
00:06:36,389 --> 00:06:37,396
It sounded generous.

106
00:06:37,819 --> 00:06:44,745
Here why it was not So there are
three estates and each gets one vote.

107
00:06:46,089 --> 00:06:47,239
Correcto.

108
00:06:47,713 --> 00:06:49,671
Ancient and fair.

109
00:06:50,914 --> 00:06:54,821
Skeptical, but we're
ninety-eight percent of France.

110
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,926
And you get one vote.

111
00:06:58,716 --> 00:06:59,826
And the clergy?

112
00:07:00,729 --> 00:07:01,537
One vote.

113
00:07:02,179 --> 00:07:04,792
And you, one vote.

114
00:07:05,684 --> 00:07:09,386
So two percent of the country
controls two-thirds of the votes.

115
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:13,592
It's tradition It's maths.

116
00:07:14,286 --> 00:07:16,623
Tradition is just maths
has been around longer.

117
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:18,956
The Third Estate refused to play along.

118
00:07:19,144 --> 00:07:22,252
On 17th June, it declared
itself the National Assembly,

119
00:07:22,596 --> 00:07:23,869
the voice of the nation.

120
00:07:23,966 --> 00:07:26,919
Three days later, it found its
meeting hall locked and guarded.

121
00:07:27,456 --> 00:07:30,668
So the deputies marched to an indoor
tennis court nearby and swore an

122
00:07:30,678 --> 00:07:34,043
oath not to leave until France had a
constitution, which is just written

123
00:07:34,043 --> 00:07:35,581
rule book for how a country is run.

124
00:07:36,017 --> 00:07:40,151
That's the Tennis Court Oath, 20
June 1789, the moment the people

125
00:07:40,162 --> 00:07:42,196
stopped asking and started claiming.

126
00:07:42,669 --> 00:07:45,914
The king's answer was to move troops
towards Paris and sack the popular

127
00:07:45,916 --> 00:07:47,786
finance minister, Jacques Necker.

128
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:52,207
On 11th July, Paris heard the
soldiers are coming and exploded.

129
00:07:52,464 --> 00:07:56,757
On 14th July, a crowd hunting for
gunpowder and weapons went to Bastille, a

130
00:07:56,757 --> 00:07:58,998
royal fortress prison in east of the city.

131
00:07:59,198 --> 00:08:01,895
And here's the part that broke
me when I was researching this.

132
00:08:02,129 --> 00:08:07,183
The Bastille held exactly seven prisoners,
four forgers, two men just insane, and

133
00:08:07,183 --> 00:08:09,267
one nobleman locked up by his own family.

134
00:08:09,554 --> 00:08:11,067
That was the great symbol of tyranny.

135
00:08:11,635 --> 00:08:13,603
Seven men and a lot of gunpowder.

136
00:08:14,067 --> 00:08:16,152
Around 100 attackers died in the fighting.

137
00:08:16,364 --> 00:08:19,512
The governor, Bernard de
Launay, was killed and his

138
00:08:19,628 --> 00:08:21,456
head was paraded on a spike.

139
00:08:22,048 --> 00:08:24,113
That was day one of liberty.

140
00:08:24,176 --> 00:08:25,978
And the king, we know what he wrote.

141
00:08:26,631 --> 00:08:28,854
Sire, the Bastille has fallen.

142
00:08:29,394 --> 00:08:30,751
Is it a revolt?

143
00:08:31,167 --> 00:08:31,773
No, sire.

144
00:08:32,198 --> 00:08:33,168
It is a revolution.

145
00:08:33,790 --> 00:08:35,323
Did I shoot anything today?

146
00:08:35,804 --> 00:08:36,405
No, sire.

147
00:08:37,009 --> 00:08:38,256
Then I shall write nothing.

148
00:08:38,721 --> 00:08:41,112
Sire, the capital is in
the hands of the crowd.

149
00:08:41,831 --> 00:08:43,732
Nothing and a note about the weather.

150
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:45,111
Pause.

151
00:08:45,487 --> 00:08:49,592
If you want the rest of this, the terror,
the emperor, and why France is on its

152
00:08:49,633 --> 00:08:54,576
fifth republic, subscribe genuinely
helps, and it costs less than a dollar.

153
00:08:55,006 --> 00:08:55,356
Yeah.

154
00:08:55,665 --> 00:08:59,601
So in August, the revolution
wrote its mission statement.

155
00:08:59,702 --> 00:09:03,549
On the night of 4th August, the nobles
gave up their feudal privileges, and

156
00:09:03,549 --> 00:09:07,663
on 26th August, the assembly adopted
the Declaration of the Rights of Man

157
00:09:07,663 --> 00:09:11,792
and Ci- and of the Citizen, which says
people are born free and equal in rights.

158
00:09:12,145 --> 00:09:14,931
It was one of the most influential
documents of the modern world,

159
00:09:15,053 --> 00:09:19,100
but rights do not fill stomachs,
and bread was still scarce.

160
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:24,681
So on 5th October 1789, thousands
of Paris women, many of them market

161
00:09:24,684 --> 00:09:29,134
sellers, marched about 12 miles in the
rain to Versailles demanding bread.

162
00:09:29,288 --> 00:09:32,326
By the next day, they had brought
the royal family back to Paris,

163
00:09:32,375 --> 00:09:34,177
effectively as the guests of the people.

164
00:09:34,646 --> 00:09:36,120
Keep that image in your back pocket.

165
00:09:36,194 --> 00:09:40,148
When prices spike and the
government seems deaf, the crowd

166
00:09:40,148 --> 00:09:42,387
goes to where the power sleeps.

167
00:09:42,518 --> 00:09:44,414
And notice the month, October.

168
00:09:44,889 --> 00:09:48,048
For two years, the king and the
revolution tried to share one country.

169
00:09:48,569 --> 00:09:52,655
It ended with a midnight escape attempt
in disguise, and the king pretending

170
00:09:52,657 --> 00:09:54,389
to be somebody's servant on it.

171
00:09:59,071 --> 00:10:04,036
Night of the 20th June 1791, Louis,
Marie, and their children slipped

172
00:10:04,038 --> 00:10:05,967
out of the Tuileries Palace in Paris.

173
00:10:06,459 --> 00:10:10,045
The king dressed as a valet, heading
for loyal troops near the border.

174
00:10:10,350 --> 00:10:11,483
They made it most of the way.

175
00:10:11,718 --> 00:10:14,810
Then, at a town called
Varennes, they were stopped.

176
00:10:14,910 --> 00:10:18,775
Story says a postmaster recognized the
king from his portrait on a banknote.

177
00:10:19,137 --> 00:10:20,665
Historians argue about the details.

178
00:10:20,732 --> 00:10:22,203
They don't argue about the result.

179
00:10:22,615 --> 00:10:25,540
The royal family was marched
back to Paris in silence.

180
00:10:25,780 --> 00:10:27,273
This mattered more than any speech.

181
00:10:27,326 --> 00:10:30,847
Until then, most French people
wanted a king under a constitution.

182
00:10:31,002 --> 00:10:34,478
After Varennes, many decided the
king had never believed any of it.

183
00:10:34,858 --> 00:10:37,952
The idea of no king at all
stopped being unthinkable.

184
00:10:38,467 --> 00:10:39,714
Then came the war.

185
00:10:40,414 --> 00:10:44,577
In April 1792, France
declared war on Austria.

186
00:10:45,087 --> 00:10:48,014
That summer, the commander of
Austrian and Parisian armies

187
00:10:48,201 --> 00:10:50,908
threatened Paris with destruction
if the royal family was harmed.

188
00:10:51,416 --> 00:10:53,007
It backfired spectacularly.

189
00:10:53,241 --> 00:10:56,968
On 10th August 1792, a
crowd stormed the Tuileries.

190
00:10:57,228 --> 00:11:00,807
Hundreds of king's Swiss Guard were
killed, and the monarchy was suspended.

191
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:03,007
By September, France was a republic.

192
00:11:03,350 --> 00:11:08,341
At this trial, the king was stripped of
his titles, called Citizen Louis Capet.

193
00:11:08,551 --> 00:11:09,434
There is that name again.

194
00:11:09,795 --> 00:11:16,021
On 21 January 1793, he was guillotined
in what is now the Place de la Concorde.

195
00:11:17,064 --> 00:11:19,173
Marie followed on 16th October.

196
00:11:19,529 --> 00:11:20,369
October again.

197
00:11:20,715 --> 00:11:24,949
With the king dead, every monarchy
in Europe was at war with France.

198
00:11:25,378 --> 00:11:26,952
There were uprisings inside the country.

199
00:11:27,065 --> 00:11:29,487
Prices were spiraling, and fear took over.

200
00:11:29,839 --> 00:11:33,558
The revolution needed someone to protect
it, and it found a small-town lawyer

201
00:11:33,631 --> 00:11:37,556
who did not drink, did not gamble,
and was nicknamed The Incorruptible.

202
00:11:37,654 --> 00:11:41,656
The government he dominated would
sentence around seventeen thousand

203
00:11:41,687 --> 00:11:43,628
people to death in just ten months.

204
00:11:43,809 --> 00:11:46,161
His name was Maximilien Robespierre.

205
00:11:46,424 --> 00:11:47,357
And here's the twist.

206
00:11:47,563 --> 00:11:51,072
In 1791, he had given speeches
arguing that France should

207
00:11:51,076 --> 00:11:52,655
abolish the death penalty.

208
00:11:52,856 --> 00:11:55,584
Two years later, he was the most
powerful member of the Committee of

209
00:11:55,585 --> 00:11:59,077
Public Safety, which ran the country
in a permanent state of emergency.

210
00:11:59,567 --> 00:12:04,099
In September 1793, the law of suspects
allowed people to be arrested not for

211
00:12:04,099 --> 00:12:08,788
what they had done, but for just being
suspected for opposing the revolution.

212
00:12:09,012 --> 00:12:10,787
Revolutionary tribunals followed.

213
00:12:11,227 --> 00:12:15,386
In June 1794, a new law stripped
many of the accused to the

214
00:12:15,642 --> 00:12:16,908
right to a defense lawyer.

215
00:12:17,139 --> 00:12:21,023
This period is called The
Terror, and the numbers are grim.

216
00:12:21,363 --> 00:12:24,836
Around seventeen thousand official
death sentences, and the historians

217
00:12:24,867 --> 00:12:29,221
estimate tens of thousands more people
died without trial or in prison.

218
00:12:29,435 --> 00:12:32,611
And most of those execution
were not aristocrats.

219
00:12:32,806 --> 00:12:36,812
They were ordinary people,
farmers, workers, shopkeepers.

220
00:12:37,191 --> 00:12:41,330
The guillotine, by the way, was meant
to be the humane option, a quick death

221
00:12:41,590 --> 00:12:43,840
that was the same for rich and the poor.

222
00:12:44,038 --> 00:12:47,609
Dr. Guillotin, whose name it
carries, did not build it, and

223
00:12:47,609 --> 00:12:49,831
he died in his bed in 1814.

224
00:12:50,204 --> 00:12:54,696
The end came on 9
Thermidor, 27th July 1794.

225
00:12:54,858 --> 00:12:58,620
Deputies feared that they
were next on Robespierre.

226
00:12:58,813 --> 00:13:02,180
He was arrested, tried to shoot
himself and shattered his jaw,

227
00:13:02,398 --> 00:13:06,659
and was guillotined the next day
with about twenty of his allies.

228
00:13:06,768 --> 00:13:10,379
The man who built the machine of
fear was eventually caught in it.

229
00:13:11,404 --> 00:13:14,091
We have replaced the seven-day
week with a ten-day week.

230
00:13:14,439 --> 00:13:14,995
Wonderful.

231
00:13:15,115 --> 00:13:16,032
And the day off?

232
00:13:16,406 --> 00:13:17,230
One day in ten.

233
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:18,831
Before it was one in seven.

234
00:13:19,202 --> 00:13:20,105
So I work more.

235
00:13:20,453 --> 00:13:21,746
You work more rationally.

236
00:13:22,198 --> 00:13:22,859
And this month?

237
00:13:23,336 --> 00:13:24,012
Fremere.

238
00:13:24,106 --> 00:13:25,117
It means frosty.

239
00:13:25,479 --> 00:13:26,256
It's raining.

240
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:28,220
It is frosty in spirit.

241
00:13:29,869 --> 00:13:32,874
Quick pause, and stay with me
because everything so far is the

242
00:13:32,875 --> 00:13:34,445
part you might have heard before.

243
00:13:34,609 --> 00:13:38,575
In about fifteen minutes, we land in
this very month, and I'm going to show

244
00:13:38,575 --> 00:13:42,958
you the three ingredients historians
say every revolution needs and exactly

245
00:13:42,958 --> 00:13:45,572
how many boxes France had ticked today.

246
00:13:46,398 --> 00:13:48,654
The answer isn't the
one headline gives you.

247
00:13:49,268 --> 00:13:52,768
But first, France was exhausted,
broke, and sick of ideology.

248
00:13:53,191 --> 00:13:57,183
It wanted order, and an artillery officer
from an island France had bought a year

249
00:13:57,189 --> 00:13:59,694
before he was born was about to offer it.

250
00:14:02,151 --> 00:14:04,291
His name was Napoleon Bonaparte.

251
00:14:04,925 --> 00:14:09,796
In November 1799, he took power
in a coup, and in 1804, he crowned

252
00:14:09,796 --> 00:14:13,078
himself emperor, literally taking
the crown from pope's hands.

253
00:14:13,719 --> 00:14:17,697
A revolution that began by beheading
a king had ended with an emperor.

254
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:20,616
But he also built things
that are still there.

255
00:14:20,872 --> 00:14:24,784
The Civil Code of 1804 is still
the foundation of French law.

256
00:14:25,132 --> 00:14:31,856
And in 1800, he created the prefects,
officials who each run region on behalf

257
00:14:31,856 --> 00:14:33,176
of the central government in Paris.

258
00:14:33,199 --> 00:14:34,188
Remember the prefects.

259
00:14:34,279 --> 00:14:34,861
They come back.

260
00:14:35,182 --> 00:14:37,423
And the legend that he was tiny.

261
00:14:37,569 --> 00:14:41,315
He was about five foot six or
seven, normal for his time.

262
00:14:41,559 --> 00:14:45,238
The myths come from French and English
inches being different lengths and

263
00:14:45,241 --> 00:14:47,193
some very effective British cartoons.

264
00:14:47,834 --> 00:14:53,786
He lost at Waterloo on 18th June
1815, and the Bourbon kings came back.

265
00:14:54,002 --> 00:14:55,733
Then France did something remarkable.

266
00:14:55,857 --> 00:14:57,961
It started overthrowing
them on a schedule.

267
00:14:58,617 --> 00:15:03,901
In July 1830, three days of street
fighting toppled Charles X, who

268
00:15:03,904 --> 00:15:05,651
had tried to muzzle the press.

269
00:15:05,704 --> 00:15:07,772
In 1848, it happened again.

270
00:15:08,174 --> 00:15:12,414
The new king, Louis-Philippe, fled
to England under the name Mr. Smith.

271
00:15:12,682 --> 00:15:16,232
The second Republic elected
Napoleon nephew as president.

272
00:15:16,519 --> 00:15:21,314
The constitution barred him from second
term, so in 1851, he took power in a

273
00:15:21,314 --> 00:15:23,799
coup and became Emperor Napoleon III.

274
00:15:24,113 --> 00:15:28,532
He rebuilt Paris with wide
boulevards, partly man- many

275
00:15:28,532 --> 00:15:32,661
historians say to barricades harder
to build and troops easier to move.

276
00:15:32,991 --> 00:15:33,895
It didn't save him.

277
00:15:34,161 --> 00:15:38,224
In September 1870, he was captured
by the Parisians at Sedan, and

278
00:15:38,224 --> 00:15:39,733
the Third Republic was declared.

279
00:15:40,450 --> 00:15:44,627
Paris was besieged for four months,
and its starving citizens ate

280
00:15:44,627 --> 00:15:46,510
the zoo, including the elephants.

281
00:15:46,904 --> 00:15:51,993
Then, in March 1871, Parisians rose
against the national government and

282
00:15:51,993 --> 00:15:54,194
set up their own, the Paris Commune.

283
00:15:54,676 --> 00:15:56,069
It lasted 72 days.

284
00:15:56,386 --> 00:16:00,520
In bloody week in May, government troops
retook the city, killing somewhere

285
00:16:00,521 --> 00:16:03,164
between 10,000 and 20,000 people.

286
00:16:04,042 --> 00:16:08,834
Fires burned across Paris, including
the Tuileries Palace, and the

287
00:16:08,834 --> 00:16:13,302
same palace the king had fled
in disguised 80 years earlier.

288
00:16:13,511 --> 00:16:18,744
So, so in under a century, France
had run through two empires, two

289
00:16:18,746 --> 00:16:22,434
republics, and three kinds of
monarchy, and it was not done.

290
00:16:23,227 --> 00:16:26,232
The next 70 years would
cost it almost everything

291
00:16:28,537 --> 00:16:31,792
The World War I took around
1.4 million French soldiers.

292
00:16:32,042 --> 00:16:36,984
By some counts, more than a quarter of
French men aged 18 to 27 were killed.

293
00:16:37,338 --> 00:16:40,977
Then in 1940, Germany conquered
France in about six weeks.

294
00:16:41,546 --> 00:16:45,080
Marshall signed an armistice, a
collaborating government set up in

295
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:50,824
a spa town of Vichy, and a general
nobody had heard of, Charles de Gaulle,

296
00:16:50,992 --> 00:16:55,109
spoke on the radio from London on 18th
June and told that the French and the

297
00:16:55,110 --> 00:16:57,114
France had lost a battle, not the war.

298
00:16:58,088 --> 00:17:01,431
After liberation in
1944, France tried again.

299
00:17:01,785 --> 00:17:06,159
The Fourth Republic, born in
1946, put parliament in charge

300
00:17:06,211 --> 00:17:07,657
and kept the president weak.

301
00:17:07,985 --> 00:17:11,305
It went through more than
20 governments in 12 years.

302
00:17:11,666 --> 00:17:16,650
When a crisis over Algeria in 1958 pushed
the army towards revolt, parliament

303
00:17:16,658 --> 00:17:21,841
turned to the one man everybody trusted,
de Gaulle, wrote the constitution.

304
00:17:22,144 --> 00:17:27,236
The Fifth Republic, the one France
still has, gave the president enormous

305
00:17:27,249 --> 00:17:31,477
power, and from 1962, the president
was elected directly by the people.

306
00:17:31,953 --> 00:17:35,725
A strong president and a National
Assembly that can bring down

307
00:17:35,932 --> 00:17:37,283
the president's government.

308
00:17:37,709 --> 00:17:38,859
Remember that design.

309
00:17:38,950 --> 00:17:44,441
It matters a lot later de Gaulle
believed he had built a republic

310
00:17:44,482 --> 00:17:45,698
that could not be shaken.

311
00:17:45,875 --> 00:17:50,000
Ten years later, a rule about
who could visit the dormitory

312
00:17:50,007 --> 00:17:51,398
nearly proved him wrong.

313
00:17:52,044 --> 00:17:56,850
In the spring of 1968, students at
Nanterre, a university outside Paris,

314
00:17:56,857 --> 00:18:00,161
were angry about plenty of things,
including rules that banned men and

315
00:18:00,161 --> 00:18:02,178
women from visiting each other rooms.

316
00:18:02,382 --> 00:18:03,971
The authorities closed the campus.

317
00:18:04,392 --> 00:18:07,829
Protests spread to the Sorbonne
in the center of Paris.

318
00:18:07,943 --> 00:18:12,533
On the night of 10th May, barricades
went up across the Latin Quarter.

319
00:18:12,804 --> 00:18:16,109
Police charged, hundreds were
injured, and public sympathy

320
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:17,799
swung towards the students.

321
00:18:18,390 --> 00:18:19,509
Then the workers joined.

322
00:18:19,895 --> 00:18:24,166
A one-day strike on 13th May
became an open-ended one.

323
00:18:24,986 --> 00:18:28,123
By late May, around 10 million
workers, roughly two-thirds of

324
00:18:28,392 --> 00:18:30,472
French workforce, were on strike.

325
00:18:30,577 --> 00:18:34,845
Factories, trains, even
broadcasting, the country stopped.

326
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:38,447
Then on 29 May, de Gaulle vanished.

327
00:18:38,534 --> 00:18:41,405
For hours, nobody knew where
the president of France was.

328
00:18:41,708 --> 00:18:46,579
He had flown to Baden-Baden in Germany
to see the general commanding French

329
00:18:46,584 --> 00:18:50,120
forces there, widely understood as a
check that the army was behind him.

330
00:18:50,695 --> 00:18:54,031
The next day, he came back,
announced elections, and in June,

331
00:18:54,141 --> 00:18:55,906
his supporters won a landslide.

332
00:18:56,200 --> 00:19:00,886
The union got a deal, too, with the
minimum wage jumping by around 35%.

333
00:19:01,144 --> 00:19:03,098
That's the French pattern
worth remembering.

334
00:19:03,534 --> 00:19:07,023
A moment that looked like a
revolution ended with a pay rise

335
00:19:07,061 --> 00:19:08,613
and a landslide for the government.

336
00:19:09,131 --> 00:19:13,329
De Gaulle himself resigned less than a
year later after losing a referendum.

337
00:19:14,143 --> 00:19:17,744
General, the students want
change, the unions want raises,

338
00:19:17,761 --> 00:19:19,312
and the factories are closed.

339
00:19:19,835 --> 00:19:23,773
How can one govern a country
that has 246 kinds of cheese?

340
00:19:24,446 --> 00:19:25,896
Is that a plan?

341
00:19:26,450 --> 00:19:27,423
It is a diagnosis.

342
00:19:28,053 --> 00:19:29,242
Shall I write it down?

343
00:19:29,779 --> 00:19:31,035
Write that it is attributed to me.

344
00:19:31,429 --> 00:19:33,370
In 60 years, nobody will check.

345
00:19:33,744 --> 00:19:34,292
Quick pause.

346
00:19:34,453 --> 00:19:37,954
If this is a history you wish someone
would explain without a jargon,

347
00:19:38,053 --> 00:19:44,721
subscribe, because the second half is
where 1789 meets today This pattern

348
00:19:44,725 --> 00:19:47,839
since 1968 was not a big explosion.

349
00:19:48,440 --> 00:19:53,024
It was a series of smaller ones,
with each a different trigger.

350
00:19:53,411 --> 00:19:57,786
In October 2005, two teenagers, Zyed
Benna and Bouna Traoré, died in an

351
00:19:57,798 --> 00:20:02,906
electrical substation in the suburb of
Clichy-sous-Bois while hiding from police.

352
00:20:03,283 --> 00:20:06,077
Riots spread through poor
suburbs for three weeks.

353
00:20:06,100 --> 00:20:08,945
Nearly nine thousand vehicles
were burned, and the government

354
00:20:08,946 --> 00:20:10,836
declared a state of emergency.

355
00:20:11,359 --> 00:20:16,400
In November 2018 came the yellow vests,
named after the high-visibility vest

356
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:18,147
where every French driver must carry.

357
00:20:18,343 --> 00:20:20,192
The trigger was rise in fuel attacks.

358
00:20:20,586 --> 00:20:25,273
Every Saturday for months, protesters
filled roundabout and city centers.

359
00:20:25,694 --> 00:20:29,106
The government dropped the tax rise, but
the anger was never really about the tax.

360
00:20:29,222 --> 00:20:31,693
It was about the cost of
living and who feels it.

361
00:20:31,995 --> 00:20:36,313
In 2023, the government raised the
retirement age from sixty-two to

362
00:20:36,314 --> 00:20:39,588
sixty-four, and it pushed through
the without the final vote in the

363
00:20:39,588 --> 00:20:44,165
Assembly, using a constitutional tool
called Article forty-nine point three.

364
00:20:44,607 --> 00:20:50,502
Millions marched, and in June 2023,
a seventeen-year-old, Nahel, was shot

365
00:20:50,522 --> 00:20:54,723
dead by a police officer during a
traffic stop in Nanterre, and riots

366
00:20:54,730 --> 00:20:56,571
spread across the country for days.

367
00:20:56,807 --> 00:20:59,311
Then politics itself began a wobble.

368
00:20:59,897 --> 00:21:03,453
After a snap election in 2024,
the Assembly split into three

369
00:21:03,454 --> 00:21:05,603
blocks, none with a majority.

370
00:21:05,792 --> 00:21:07,032
Governments started falling.

371
00:21:07,156 --> 00:21:12,540
In December 2024, the Prime Minister,
Michel Barnier, lost a no-confidence vote.

372
00:21:12,609 --> 00:21:17,185
In September 2025, his successor,
François Bayrou, lost a confidence

373
00:21:17,189 --> 00:21:20,402
vote after proposing around
forty-four billion euros in savings.

374
00:21:20,726 --> 00:21:25,467
Two days later, a grassroots movement
called Block Everything shut down parts

375
00:21:25,468 --> 00:21:30,832
of the country, and Macron appointed
Sébastien Lecornu, his seventh prime

376
00:21:30,832 --> 00:21:33,482
minister since taking office in 2017.

377
00:21:34,333 --> 00:21:38,979
The triggers keep changing: a dome
rule, a fuel tax, a retirement

378
00:21:38,991 --> 00:21:41,623
age, a police shooting, a budget.

379
00:21:41,943 --> 00:21:45,522
But protesters keep describing
the same complaint: that

380
00:21:45,528 --> 00:21:49,790
decisions are made far away by the
people who don't feel the cost.

381
00:21:50,432 --> 00:21:54,387
And the governments keep giving
the same answer: that the debt is

382
00:21:54,389 --> 00:21:56,441
real and that it does not wait.

383
00:21:56,668 --> 00:21:59,909
Which bring us to the autumn of 2026.

384
00:22:00,324 --> 00:22:03,719
It began in mid-September with
two things happening at once.

385
00:22:04,014 --> 00:22:08,477
On the south coast, fishermen blockaded
the port of Nice and a fuel depot near

386
00:22:08,541 --> 00:22:10,975
city, furious about the price of diesel.

387
00:22:11,005 --> 00:22:14,877
And in the suburbs of Paris and other
cities, high school students started

388
00:22:14,896 --> 00:22:19,222
blockading their own schools over teacher
shortages, overcrowded schools, and

389
00:22:19,510 --> 00:22:21,385
buildings that can't cope with the heat.

390
00:22:21,493 --> 00:22:24,782
By early October, one analysis
counted more than seven hundred

391
00:22:24,785 --> 00:22:26,192
secondary schools disrupted.

392
00:22:26,619 --> 00:22:30,069
On seventh October, around a
quarter of a million people marched.

393
00:22:30,142 --> 00:22:30,859
Why schools?

394
00:22:31,387 --> 00:22:35,866
One union survey of more than a thousand
schools found that at the start of the

395
00:22:35,866 --> 00:22:39,825
year, fifty-three percent of secondary
schools were missing at least one teacher.

396
00:22:40,929 --> 00:22:44,678
Reports describe classes
of 35 or even 40 students.

397
00:22:44,941 --> 00:22:45,558
Then add money.

398
00:22:46,071 --> 00:22:51,641
Prices rose 3.1% in the year of
September, up from 2.4% in August.

399
00:22:51,903 --> 00:22:54,475
Diesel is around €2.37 a liter.

400
00:22:54,605 --> 00:22:57,551
Reports tie the energy
squeeze to the war in Iran.

401
00:22:57,959 --> 00:22:59,525
And then add the debt.

402
00:23:00,335 --> 00:23:05,526
France's public debt is about 119% of
its entire economy, the highest ratio

403
00:23:05,537 --> 00:23:07,257
since the aftermath of World War II.

404
00:23:07,583 --> 00:23:10,033
The deficit is 5.4% of GDP.

405
00:23:10,308 --> 00:23:13,594
The 2027 budget aims for
5%, and that means finding

406
00:23:13,595 --> 00:23:15,962
about €43 billion in savings.

407
00:23:16,628 --> 00:23:20,207
Government borrowing costs hit their
highest level in more than 20 years.

408
00:23:20,518 --> 00:23:23,388
One projection says the
interest bill alone could near

409
00:23:23,666 --> 00:23:26,878
$100 billion a year by 2027.

410
00:23:27,318 --> 00:23:28,144
Here is the collision.

411
00:23:28,395 --> 00:23:32,356
Student and teacher are demanding more
spending at the exact moment a government

412
00:23:32,357 --> 00:23:35,918
that has cut spending is trying to pass
a budget through a split parliament.

413
00:23:36,347 --> 00:23:41,487
On 17th October, the school movement
merged with revived yellow vest network

414
00:23:41,500 --> 00:23:45,869
unions, retiree, farmers, and climate
campaigners into one national day

415
00:23:45,871 --> 00:23:47,382
of protest over the cost of living.

416
00:23:47,894 --> 00:23:52,328
The interior minister counted
about 550,000 people, around

417
00:23:52,688 --> 00:23:54,901
110,000 of them in Paris.

418
00:23:55,302 --> 00:23:59,149
By Reuters' account, that was bigger than
the yellow vest at their peak and far

419
00:23:59,150 --> 00:24:01,653
smaller than the 2023 pensions protest.

420
00:24:02,062 --> 00:24:05,381
Most of them marched peacefully,
but violent groups attacked town

421
00:24:05,381 --> 00:24:08,833
halls and police stations, set
cars on fire, and looted shops.

422
00:24:09,272 --> 00:24:11,233
The city hall in Limoges was burning.

423
00:24:11,902 --> 00:24:17,282
In Bordeaux, a masked crowd forced its
way in the residence of the local prefect.

424
00:24:17,848 --> 00:24:23,410
Yes, that prefect, the office
Napoleon invented 226 years ago.

425
00:24:23,784 --> 00:24:29,477
The toll as reported, nearly 1,800 arrests
across the country, a record 855 in

426
00:24:29,480 --> 00:24:31,781
Paris, and more than 500 police injured.

427
00:24:32,107 --> 00:24:34,706
One protester died after
a cardiac arrest in Paris.

428
00:24:35,049 --> 00:24:38,939
The interior minister confirmed that
the person lost a hand in clash with

429
00:24:38,988 --> 00:24:44,054
police, and allegations of excessive force
against young protesters are synchronized.

430
00:24:44,382 --> 00:24:45,728
The response was curfews.

431
00:24:46,237 --> 00:24:48,678
Overnight curfews in two areas of Paris.

432
00:24:49,275 --> 00:24:54,318
Curfews for under 18s in cities
including Lille and Nice.

433
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:58,662
The government says the protest cooled
afterward, and another national day

434
00:24:58,663 --> 00:25:00,039
of protest is planned this weekend.

435
00:25:00,767 --> 00:25:03,840
Smaller protests around living
costs have also appeared in

436
00:25:03,853 --> 00:25:05,289
Belgium, Portugal, and Italy.

437
00:25:05,986 --> 00:25:09,566
And Moody’s, the credit rating
agencies, was due to rule on

438
00:25:09,568 --> 00:25:12,186
France debt rating on 23 October.

439
00:25:12,568 --> 00:25:14,355
So what's driving it?

440
00:25:15,108 --> 00:25:16,633
That's where people split.

441
00:25:17,982 --> 00:25:21,034
Everyone looking at the same smoke
is describing a different fire.

442
00:25:21,097 --> 00:25:24,917
So let me walk through what each camp
is actually saying in the version

443
00:25:24,976 --> 00:25:27,021
its own supporters will recognize.

444
00:25:27,646 --> 00:25:31,344
The government says a peaceful protest
was hijacked by a violent minority.

445
00:25:31,928 --> 00:25:35,129
The president said there was
no room for violence and that

446
00:25:35,159 --> 00:25:36,871
rioters would be prosecuted.

447
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:40,437
The prime minister said nobody would
impose a political agenda through

448
00:25:40,437 --> 00:25:45,123
violence and accused the far-left France
Unbowed party of using the unrest as

449
00:25:45,129 --> 00:25:46,695
warm-up for the presidential election.

450
00:25:47,114 --> 00:25:51,975
That party's leader, Jean-Luc Melenchon,
rejected the charges and told the prime

451
00:25:51,976 --> 00:25:54,897
minister to prove it or apologize.

452
00:25:55,064 --> 00:25:58,267
He has backed the student and
criticized the use of tear gas

453
00:25:58,582 --> 00:26:00,120
and batons against young people.

454
00:26:00,390 --> 00:26:04,541
On the other side, the far-right
National Rally says the government

455
00:26:04,552 --> 00:26:07,727
has failed at the most basic
job of a state keeping order.

456
00:26:08,078 --> 00:26:13,215
Its president, Jordan Bardella, called the
president's televised address lamentable.

457
00:26:13,782 --> 00:26:18,187
Marine Le Pen called the government
exhausted and incapable and said

458
00:26:18,187 --> 00:26:21,544
rioters had spoiled a demonstration
held in defense of purchasing power.

459
00:26:21,971 --> 00:26:25,956
Abroad, Donald Trump blamed
mass immigration and Islam.

460
00:26:26,716 --> 00:26:30,279
President Macron's reply was that
foreign leaders were exploiting French

461
00:26:30,282 --> 00:26:35,103
riots for their own election purpose,
with America midterms days away.

462
00:26:35,870 --> 00:26:39,581
And the economist and analyst
mostly point at three things: debt,

463
00:26:39,985 --> 00:26:41,978
prices, and underfunded schools.

464
00:26:42,533 --> 00:26:45,205
Some add the movement has
drifted from school grievances

465
00:26:45,213 --> 00:26:47,158
towards plain lawlessness.

466
00:26:47,458 --> 00:26:49,690
Each side has a point its
supporters want to hear.

467
00:26:50,253 --> 00:26:55,110
Those condemning the violence note that
a half million people marched there, that

468
00:26:55,110 --> 00:26:59,462
the people who pay for arson and looting
are shopkeepers, local workers, and public

469
00:26:59,463 --> 00:27:02,243
services, that no grievances justifies it.

470
00:27:02,789 --> 00:27:06,356
Those defending the protesters note
that the school staffing figures are

471
00:27:06,357 --> 00:27:10,753
documented, that the grievances were
real before anyone threw anything,

472
00:27:10,831 --> 00:27:13,209
and that police deserves scrutiny.

473
00:27:13,619 --> 00:27:15,895
Both of those can be true at once.

474
00:27:16,947 --> 00:27:18,906
I'm not going to tell
you which camp is right.

475
00:27:19,015 --> 00:27:22,735
What I can do is show you what
history says about a moment like this.

476
00:27:22,976 --> 00:27:27,417
But first, a look at what the people
in the middle of it are actually facing

477
00:27:30,226 --> 00:27:32,846
We need to find forty-three billion euros.

478
00:27:33,211 --> 00:27:33,973
Raise taxes?

479
00:27:34,196 --> 00:27:35,363
The wealthy will leave.

480
00:27:35,641 --> 00:27:36,258
Cut spending.

481
00:27:36,434 --> 00:27:37,064
On what?

482
00:27:37,306 --> 00:27:37,790
Schools.

483
00:27:38,074 --> 00:27:39,436
The schools are on strike.

484
00:27:39,795 --> 00:27:40,308
Pensions?

485
00:27:40,595 --> 00:27:42,981
Last time we touched pensions,
the country caught fire.

486
00:27:43,144 --> 00:27:43,784
Borrow more.

487
00:27:44,018 --> 00:27:46,517
The markets are charging us the
highest rate in twenty years.

488
00:27:46,939 --> 00:27:47,697
So what's the plan?

489
00:27:48,023 --> 00:27:48,867
We'll form a committee.

490
00:27:49,033 --> 00:27:49,465
Oh, good.

491
00:27:49,546 --> 00:27:50,001
A committee.

492
00:27:50,610 --> 00:27:53,991
The political scientist Jack
Goldstone spent a career

493
00:27:53,995 --> 00:27:55,761
studying why states break down.

494
00:27:56,036 --> 00:27:59,568
His answer was that it tends to
happen when three things line up.

495
00:27:59,823 --> 00:28:03,750
One, the state is in fiscal
crisis and can't pay its bills.

496
00:28:04,198 --> 00:28:07,493
Two, the elites are divided and
they're fighting each other.

497
00:28:07,759 --> 00:28:11,026
Three, a large part of the
population is mobilized and angry.

498
00:28:11,205 --> 00:28:12,658
Let's score both years.

499
00:28:13,158 --> 00:28:15,799
1789, fiscal crisis.

500
00:28:16,007 --> 00:28:17,802
Yes, the kingdom was bankrupt.

501
00:28:17,948 --> 00:28:18,798
Elites divided.

502
00:28:18,918 --> 00:28:21,352
Yes, the nobles blocked every reform.

503
00:28:21,501 --> 00:28:24,971
The court fought the king, and
some nobles and priests end

504
00:28:24,971 --> 00:28:26,371
up joining the third state.

505
00:28:27,033 --> 00:28:27,669
Mass anger?

506
00:28:28,007 --> 00:28:28,509
Yes.

507
00:28:29,443 --> 00:28:30,825
Hungry crowds in Paris.

508
00:28:31,029 --> 00:28:34,648
And then the fourth thing that
sealed it, the army split.

509
00:28:34,917 --> 00:28:38,154
Units of the French Guard joined
the crowd at the Bastille.

510
00:28:38,237 --> 00:28:42,037
When the people with the guns stop
obeying, a government is finished.

511
00:28:42,293 --> 00:28:47,024
2026. Fiscal crisis, arguably,
but a different kind.

512
00:28:47,234 --> 00:28:48,463
France is not bankrupt.

513
00:28:48,741 --> 00:28:50,636
Markets still lend to it.

514
00:28:51,018 --> 00:28:55,422
And one chief economist told Le Monde the
debt problem is serious but manageable.

515
00:28:55,840 --> 00:28:58,455
Unless political
instability make it worse.

516
00:28:59,327 --> 00:29:04,747
But debt at 119% of GDP and
borrowing costs at 20-year highs

517
00:29:04,779 --> 00:29:06,602
mean every budget is a war.

518
00:29:06,969 --> 00:29:07,713
Elites divide?

519
00:29:07,832 --> 00:29:09,011
Yes, in a different way.

520
00:29:09,212 --> 00:29:11,905
Parliament is split into blocs.

521
00:29:11,923 --> 00:29:13,239
Governments keep falling.

522
00:29:14,124 --> 00:29:16,850
Everyone is competing ahead of
election, but they are competing to

523
00:29:16,851 --> 00:29:19,031
win the presidency, not to abolish it.

524
00:29:19,279 --> 00:29:19,924
Mass anger?

525
00:29:20,242 --> 00:29:20,607
Yes.

526
00:29:20,778 --> 00:29:23,886
Half a million people
marched, plus students, union.

527
00:29:24,155 --> 00:29:27,356
Farmers, fishermen, but it's leaderless.

528
00:29:27,695 --> 00:29:29,331
It demands are mixed.

529
00:29:29,803 --> 00:29:34,472
From school to diesel to wages and a
violent fringe that most marchers did

530
00:29:34,473 --> 00:29:36,544
not join has taken much of the attention.

531
00:29:36,996 --> 00:29:38,483
Now, look at what is missing.

532
00:29:38,803 --> 00:29:40,628
One, no defection.

533
00:29:40,910 --> 00:29:45,775
The police and the army are doing their
jobs, where in 1789 they switched sides.

534
00:29:46,275 --> 00:29:48,381
Two, no alternate system, no offer.

535
00:29:48,926 --> 00:29:51,144
Nobody is marching to
abolish the republic.

536
00:29:51,430 --> 00:29:55,280
Even the loudest critics want to win
the election that's already scheduled.

537
00:29:55,870 --> 00:30:00,450
In 1789, the alternative to the king
had to be invented from scratch.

538
00:30:00,859 --> 00:30:05,524
And three, a legal exit
exists about six months away.

539
00:30:05,817 --> 00:30:08,833
In 1789, the only road to
change was a meeting that the

540
00:30:08,835 --> 00:30:10,189
king did not want to hold.

541
00:30:10,692 --> 00:30:13,099
But there are echoes that
go beyond the checklist.

542
00:30:13,286 --> 00:30:17,705
In October 1789, a crowd went
to Versailles because that

543
00:30:17,705 --> 00:30:18,883
is where the power slept.

544
00:30:19,124 --> 00:30:24,150
In October 2026, crowd went
to town hall and prefectures.

545
00:30:24,460 --> 00:30:29,937
And in 1789, the summer was swept by the
great fear rumors that aristocrats had

546
00:30:29,942 --> 00:30:32,635
hired brigands to starve the villages.

547
00:30:32,994 --> 00:30:36,204
Peasants armed themselves against
the enemies who did not exist.

548
00:30:36,687 --> 00:30:40,752
In 2026, every camp suspects
everyone's hidden hand, and

549
00:30:40,753 --> 00:30:42,508
foreign leaders have weighed in.

550
00:30:43,115 --> 00:30:46,962
When truth collapses, every explanation
starts to sound like a plot.

551
00:30:47,376 --> 00:30:52,098
So is France in a revolution by
the historians' own checklist?

552
00:30:52,427 --> 00:30:53,003
Not yet.

553
00:30:53,493 --> 00:30:56,824
And yes, the word yet is
doing a lot of work here.

554
00:30:57,396 --> 00:31:00,635
But historians are very bad at
predicting revolutions and very

555
00:31:00,635 --> 00:31:02,091
good at explaining them afterwards.

556
00:31:02,684 --> 00:31:08,930
In May 1968, nobody on the barricades knew
the system would hold either until it did.

557
00:31:09,818 --> 00:31:15,486
What 2026 shares with 1789 is the cause,
the question of who pays when the bill

558
00:31:15,522 --> 00:31:18,274
comes due, not so far the outcome.

559
00:31:18,570 --> 00:31:22,424
And that question is almost
two and a half centuries old.

